### What is the Sliding Window Maximum problem?

Given an array of integers and a window size K, return an array where each element is the maximum of the contiguous window of K elements ending at that position (once the window is fully formed). The window slides from left to right one position at a time.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class SlidingWindowMaximum {
    public static int[] maxSlidingWindow(int[] nums, int k) {
        if (nums == null || nums.length == 0) return new int[0];
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] result = new int[n - k + 1];
        Deque<Integer> deque = new ArrayDeque<>();  // stores indices

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            // Remove from back: smaller values are now useless
            while (!deque.isEmpty() && nums[deque.peekLast()] <= nums[i]) {
                deque.pollLast();
            }
            deque.offerLast(i);

            // Remove from front: index out of window
            if (deque.peekFirst() <= i - k) {
                deque.pollFirst();
            }

            // Record the maximum once window is full
            if (i >= k - 1) {
                result[i - k + 1] = nums[deque.peekFirst()];
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] rates = {10, 5, 12, 8, 15, 3, 7};
        int k = 3;
        int[] maxes = maxSlidingWindow(rates, k);
        System.out.print("Window maximums: ");
        for (int m : maxes) System.out.print(m + " ");
    }
}
```

```text
Window maximums: 12 12 15 15 15
```

### Why store indices instead of values?

Evicting the front of the deque depends on knowing whether an element has slid out of the window. You can only determine that by comparing the index with the current window boundary (i - k). If you stored values instead of indices, you would not know when to evict the front.

```java
// Front eviction uses the INDEX:
if (deque.peekFirst() <= i - k) deque.pollFirst();

// If we stored values instead:
// deque.peekFirst() returns a value like 10
// We cannot compare 10 <= i - k (meaningless)
```

### How does the decreasing invariant work?

Before adding a new index, you pop from the back every index whose value is less than or equal to the new value. These elements can never be the maximum of any future window because the new element is both larger and more recent. After this cleanup, the deque's values are strictly decreasing from front to back.

```java
// Walkthrough: nums = [10, 5, 12, 8, 15], k=3
// i=0: deque=[], push 0 -> deque=[0] (values: [10])
// i=1: nums[0]=10 > 5, push 1 -> deque=[0,1] (values: [10,5])
// i=2: nums[1]=5 <= 12, pop 1; nums[0]=10 <= 12, pop 0
//      push 2 -> deque=[2] (values: [12])
//      i >= k-1, result[0] = nums[2] = 12
// i=3: nums[2]=12 > 8, push 3 -> deque=[2,3] (values: [12,8])
//      front=2, 2 <= 3-3=0? No. result[1] = nums[2] = 12
// i=4: nums[3]=8 <= 15, pop 3; nums[2]=12 <= 15, pop 2
//      push 4 -> deque=[4] (values: [15])
//      result[2] = nums[4] = 15
```

### How is this different from a naive approach?

The naive approach scans each window for its maximum, giving O(n*K) time. A heap-based approach gives O(n log K) because removing expired elements costs log time. The monotonic deque achieves O(n) because each index enters and leaves at most once.

| Approach | Time | Space | Key Property |
|---|---|---|---|
| Naive scan | O(n*K) | O(1) | Recomputes each window |
| Max-heap | O(n log K) | O(K) | Lazy removal from heap |
| Monotonic deque | O(n) | O(K) | Each index enters/leaves once |

```java
// Naive O(n*K):
for (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {
    int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
    for (int j = i; j < i + k; j++) max = Math.max(max, nums[j]);
    result[i] = max;
}
```

### What about using <= vs < for back eviction?

Using <= (less than or equal) on the back eviction means equal values are also popped. This is correct because if two elements have the same value, the newer one is strictly better: it stays in the window longer. Using strict < would leave stale equal-valued indices that serve no purpose.

```java
// Correct: use <= to pop equal values too
while (!deque.isEmpty() && nums[deque.peekLast()] <= nums[i]) {
    deque.pollLast();
}
// Equal values from earlier are now useless because the newer one
// has the same value but stays in the window longer
```

### What are the edge cases?

A window of size 1 returns the array itself. A window equal to the array length returns a single-element result (the global maximum). An empty array returns an empty result.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SlidingWindowMaxEdge {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // k = 1: each element is its own maximum
        int[] r1 = SlidingWindowMaximum.maxSlidingWindow(new int[]{3, 1, 4}, 1);
        for (int v : r1) System.out.print(v + " "); // 3 1 4
        System.out.println();

        // k = array length: single result = global max
        int[] r2 = SlidingWindowMaximum.maxSlidingWindow(new int[]{3, 1, 4}, 3);
        for (int v : r2) System.out.print(v + " "); // 4
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

```text
3 1 4
4
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting the front eviction check, which leaves expired indices in the deque and reports a maximum from outside the current window.

```java
// WRONG: no front eviction
deque.offerLast(i);
// Missing: if (deque.peekFirst() <= i - k) deque.pollFirst();
// This can report a maximum that is no longer in the window

// CORRECT: always check front eviction
deque.offerLast(i);
if (deque.peekFirst() <= i - k) deque.pollFirst();
```

### When should you use a monotonic deque?

Use a monotonic deque whenever you need the maximum (or minimum) of a sliding window in linear time. In banking, this applies to tracking rolling peak transaction rates, peak balances, or peak load over a sliding time window.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class PeakRateMonitor {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] perSecondRates = {45, 60, 33, 72, 55, 88, 41, 67};
        int windowSize = 4;
        int[] peaks = SlidingWindowMaximum.maxSlidingWindow(perSecondRates, windowSize);
        System.out.print("Rolling peak rates: ");
        for (int p : peaks) System.out.print(p + " ");
    }
}
```

```text
Rolling peak rates: 72 72 88 88 88
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should explain the amortized O(n) analysis: each of the n indices is pushed once and popped at most once, giving at most 2n deque operations total. The deque never holds more than K indices, bounding the space. The decreasing invariant guarantees the front is always the maximum, and the back-eviction with <= (not <) prevents stale equal-valued duplicates.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers commonly ask:
- Why store indices, not values? (Front eviction depends on position.)
- Why <= not <? (Equal-valued older elements are useless since the newer one stays longer.)
- Time complexity? (O(n) amortized, each index pushed and popped at most once.)
- Can you compute sliding window minimum? (Same deque, but keep increasing order and pop back when value >= new.)
